WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:09.830 --> 00:00:11.530
MINHAZ KAZI: Welcome
to Cloud OnAir,

00:00:11.530 --> 00:00:13.360
live webinars from Google Cloud.

00:00:13.360 --> 00:00:15.890
We are hosting
webinars every Tuesday.

00:00:15.890 --> 00:00:17.870
You can ask questions
anytime on the platform,

00:00:17.870 --> 00:00:20.110
and we have Googlers on
the standby to answer them.

00:00:20.110 --> 00:00:21.640
I'm Minhaz Kazi,
developer advocate

00:00:21.640 --> 00:00:22.672
for Google Data Studio.

00:00:22.672 --> 00:00:24.880
MATT HAMRICK: And I'm Matt
Hamrick, developer program

00:00:24.880 --> 00:00:26.109
engineer for Data Studio.

00:00:26.109 --> 00:00:27.400
MINHAZ KAZI: Let's get started.

00:00:31.220 --> 00:00:34.910
So Data Studio is Google's
business intelligence and data

00:00:34.910 --> 00:00:36.440
visualization platform.

00:00:36.440 --> 00:00:38.540
In this presentation,
we will cover,

00:00:38.540 --> 00:00:42.110
what is Data Studio, who can
and should use Data Studio,

00:00:42.110 --> 00:00:44.210
how can you connect your
data in Data Studio,

00:00:44.210 --> 00:00:47.060
and how can you share the
dashboards that you create?

00:00:47.060 --> 00:00:49.050
Before we go into
our presentation,

00:00:49.050 --> 00:00:52.650
let's try to see who would
be interested in using

00:00:52.650 --> 00:00:53.319
Data Studio.

00:00:53.319 --> 00:00:54.860
Maybe you're are an
analyst who wants

00:00:54.860 --> 00:00:57.440
to monitor internal business
metrics of a business.

00:00:57.440 --> 00:00:59.980
Or maybe you are a developer
who is working with a client,

00:00:59.980 --> 00:01:01.700
and the solution
you are building

00:01:01.700 --> 00:01:04.250
needs a dashboard
built on top of it.

00:01:04.250 --> 00:01:07.280
Maybe you are an executive who
would like to present results

00:01:07.280 --> 00:01:12.050
in the meeting for maybe a
quarterly results or maybe

00:01:12.050 --> 00:01:13.730
all-hands results.

00:01:13.730 --> 00:01:16.250
Or maybe you are a
CTO of an organization

00:01:16.250 --> 00:01:18.620
who would like to
provide additional value

00:01:18.620 --> 00:01:19.886
to your customers.

00:01:19.886 --> 00:01:21.260
Or maybe you are
a data scientist

00:01:21.260 --> 00:01:23.330
who had just done
some great research

00:01:23.330 --> 00:01:25.130
and have some
findings, and you'd

00:01:25.130 --> 00:01:27.370
like to publish these
findings on a blog post

00:01:27.370 --> 00:01:30.436
so that everyone can see those
and play around with the data.

00:01:30.436 --> 00:01:32.060
Or maybe you're just
owner of some data

00:01:32.060 --> 00:01:34.070
and would like to
visualize that.

00:01:34.070 --> 00:01:36.680
In most of these cases,
the solution that you

00:01:36.680 --> 00:01:39.500
need to work with, the
visualization platform

00:01:39.500 --> 00:01:42.350
will need to be scalable,
interactive, cost-effective,

00:01:42.350 --> 00:01:44.150
and high-performing.

00:01:44.150 --> 00:01:47.150
And all of these cases go
very well with Data Studio,

00:01:47.150 --> 00:01:51.530
because it is a visualization
and reporting platform that

00:01:51.530 --> 00:01:53.090
is completely serverless.

00:01:53.090 --> 00:01:55.070
It runs on Google's Cloud.

00:01:55.070 --> 00:01:59.300
You can build engaging reports
and dashboards in the platform.

00:01:59.300 --> 00:02:01.040
You can leverage
teamwork that works,

00:02:01.040 --> 00:02:03.560
and you can use existing
connectors and templates.

00:02:03.560 --> 00:02:06.260
And also, Data Studio is
completely free to use.

00:02:06.260 --> 00:02:09.740
You don't have to pay
anything to use it.

00:02:09.740 --> 00:02:11.880
Now, let's see what
it can actually do.

00:02:11.880 --> 00:02:15.120
We'll have a look at a
few example dashboards.

00:02:15.120 --> 00:02:20.740
The first one over here shows
us some website performance.

00:02:20.740 --> 00:02:22.850
And this is a live
dashboard, as you can see.

00:02:22.850 --> 00:02:24.790
It's pulling in data
from Google Analytics'

00:02:24.790 --> 00:02:27.024
account of the Google
Merchandise Store.

00:02:27.024 --> 00:02:28.690
You can pick different
date ranges here.

00:02:28.690 --> 00:02:30.270
You can pick user type.

00:02:30.270 --> 00:02:32.750
And this live dashboard shows
different kinds of metrics

00:02:32.750 --> 00:02:36.150
that you can use to evaluate
even your own website using

00:02:36.150 --> 00:02:38.570
the same template.

00:02:38.570 --> 00:02:42.040
Now, let's have a look
at the second dashboard,

00:02:42.040 --> 00:02:44.500
and this is an
economic dashboard

00:02:44.500 --> 00:02:48.580
which talks about the Economic
Freedom Index of the world.

00:02:48.580 --> 00:02:50.560
Over here on the
left side, you'll

00:02:50.560 --> 00:02:53.500
see that there is an
overall score distribution.

00:02:53.500 --> 00:02:55.690
And then, there is
the distribution

00:02:55.690 --> 00:02:58.564
of all the other scores
that make up the index.

00:02:58.564 --> 00:03:00.480
And as you hover over
each of the data points,

00:03:00.480 --> 00:03:03.280
it will show you the detailed
information about each data

00:03:03.280 --> 00:03:05.850
point.

00:03:05.850 --> 00:03:08.380
And to have a look at
the last dashboard,

00:03:08.380 --> 00:03:11.770
this is, of course, a
FIFA World Cup leaderboard

00:03:11.770 --> 00:03:14.620
where you can see results of
different games of the FIFA

00:03:14.620 --> 00:03:18.000
World Cup and how the
whole game progressed.

00:03:18.000 --> 00:03:20.050
If I look at the Live
Knockout Bracket,

00:03:20.050 --> 00:03:22.920
you can see over here,
as the data loads,

00:03:22.920 --> 00:03:27.738
the results of every match from
the round of 16 to the finals.

00:03:27.738 --> 00:03:29.620
Now, these are some
examples of what

00:03:29.620 --> 00:03:32.629
you can do with Data Studio.

00:03:32.629 --> 00:03:34.420
The way Data Studio
works is that first you

00:03:34.420 --> 00:03:35.330
connect your data.

00:03:35.330 --> 00:03:37.009
Then you analyze
and visualize it.

00:03:37.009 --> 00:03:38.800
And then once you have
built the dashboard,

00:03:38.800 --> 00:03:41.450
you can share it
with other people.

00:03:41.450 --> 00:03:43.960
So when you're
connecting your data,

00:03:43.960 --> 00:03:45.970
the way you connect
your data in Data Studio

00:03:45.970 --> 00:03:47.860
is you use connectors.

00:03:47.860 --> 00:03:49.960
There are connectors
available that

00:03:49.960 --> 00:03:53.870
let you use data from Google
Sheets or other Cloud services,

00:03:53.870 --> 00:03:57.670
like BigQuery, Cloud SQL, Cloud
Storage, or Cloud Spanner.

00:03:57.670 --> 00:04:01.300
If you have on-premise data
in maybe PostgresSQL or MySQL,

00:04:01.300 --> 00:04:02.980
you can also bring them in.

00:04:02.980 --> 00:04:05.920
Furthermore, you can connect
to other Google services,

00:04:05.920 --> 00:04:08.650
like YouTube, Analytics,
AdWords, DoubleClick, Search

00:04:08.650 --> 00:04:11.080
Console, et cetera.

00:04:11.080 --> 00:04:12.620
But here's the problem.

00:04:12.620 --> 00:04:15.190
What if your data is not
in any of these sources?

00:04:15.190 --> 00:04:20.050
What if your data is in some
kind of maybe external website

00:04:20.050 --> 00:04:21.519
or in a social platform?

00:04:21.519 --> 00:04:24.850
Or maybe its on premise
hidden behind some kind

00:04:24.850 --> 00:04:26.110
of authentication method?

00:04:26.110 --> 00:04:27.420
What do you do then?

00:04:27.420 --> 00:04:29.170
That's where we have
Community Connectors,

00:04:29.170 --> 00:04:30.790
and Matt will talk
more about it.

00:04:30.790 --> 00:04:31.498
MATT HAMRICK: Hi.

00:04:31.498 --> 00:04:32.124
Thanks, Minhaz.

00:04:32.124 --> 00:04:33.790
So community connectors
are the solution

00:04:33.790 --> 00:04:35.500
that we have in Data
Studio that allows

00:04:35.500 --> 00:04:38.170
you to connect to your own
data that's not necessarily

00:04:38.170 --> 00:04:40.710
in Google.

00:04:40.710 --> 00:04:43.622
So Community Connectors has
three main points do it.

00:04:43.622 --> 00:04:45.830
It's built in App Script,
which is Google's scripting

00:04:45.830 --> 00:04:49.310
platform that works across all
of the different Google apps.

00:04:49.310 --> 00:04:51.140
It's low effort to develop.

00:04:51.140 --> 00:04:53.210
You only need to
define a few functions.

00:04:53.210 --> 00:04:54.740
And it has a share
and publish model

00:04:54.740 --> 00:04:58.880
that should already be familiar
to anyone who's used Drive.

00:04:58.880 --> 00:05:01.610
To develop a Connector, you
have these three main methods

00:05:01.610 --> 00:05:04.520
that you have to implement,
getConfig, getSchema,

00:05:04.520 --> 00:05:05.750
and getData.

00:05:05.750 --> 00:05:07.550
getConfig, the first
one, is just a way

00:05:07.550 --> 00:05:10.160
that you can say these are
the configuration options that

00:05:10.160 --> 00:05:12.800
are going to be necessary in
order for my data to actually

00:05:12.800 --> 00:05:13.925
be connected to.

00:05:13.925 --> 00:05:16.827
getSchema just
provides the fields

00:05:16.827 --> 00:05:19.160
that would be necessary to
say these are the fields that

00:05:19.160 --> 00:05:20.900
make sense for my data source.

00:05:20.900 --> 00:05:24.290
And getData is the actual fetch
to the data source itself.

00:05:24.290 --> 00:05:27.035
So this might be talking to
some sort of SQL database,

00:05:27.035 --> 00:05:30.230
or it can alternatively just be
making like a simple HTTP call.

00:05:32.880 --> 00:05:36.840
So I'm going to do a quick demo
showing a Community Connector.

00:05:36.840 --> 00:05:39.035
This one is for Stack Overflow.

00:05:42.930 --> 00:05:45.390
So what you'll do when
you go into Data Studio

00:05:45.390 --> 00:05:47.960
is you will have the ability
to make a connector to any data

00:05:47.960 --> 00:05:51.994
source here, so I'm just
going to search for stack.

00:05:51.994 --> 00:05:53.410
And you'll see
here at the bottom,

00:05:53.410 --> 00:05:54.540
we have Stack
Overflow Questions.

00:05:54.540 --> 00:05:56.165
That's the one I want
to use, so I just

00:05:56.165 --> 00:05:57.977
click Create Data Source.

00:05:57.977 --> 00:06:00.060
And that takes us to this
next page, where we will

00:06:00.060 --> 00:06:02.160
be configuring the connector.

00:06:02.160 --> 00:06:04.330
So you can see that
there's a few options here.

00:06:04.330 --> 00:06:06.180
It says, which tag
would I like to do,

00:06:06.180 --> 00:06:09.450
how many results do I want,
and what sort should it do?

00:06:09.450 --> 00:06:11.760
So I'm going to use the
predefined tag of Google Data

00:06:11.760 --> 00:06:12.260
Studio.

00:06:18.735 --> 00:06:20.360
And then I'm going
to go ahead, and I'm

00:06:20.360 --> 00:06:23.300
going to do a
results count of 22.

00:06:23.300 --> 00:06:26.754
And I'm going to sort by votes.

00:06:26.754 --> 00:06:28.170
You'll also see
here at the bottom

00:06:28.170 --> 00:06:30.670
that there's a thing checked
that says, "use report template

00:06:30.670 --> 00:06:31.470
for new reports."

00:06:31.470 --> 00:06:33.570
This is just provided by
the connector's creator,

00:06:33.570 --> 00:06:35.730
and it's basically a way
to get you kickstarted,

00:06:35.730 --> 00:06:37.271
where once you have
this data source,

00:06:37.271 --> 00:06:40.200
it will give you something
that looks good already.

00:06:40.200 --> 00:06:42.670
So we click Connect, and
we see this field screen.

00:06:42.670 --> 00:06:44.795
You'll see that there's
quite a few fields that are

00:06:44.795 --> 00:06:47.420
provided from this connector.

00:06:47.420 --> 00:06:50.790
You have question counts,
links, activity aids, et cetera.

00:06:50.790 --> 00:06:53.847
So I'm going to go ahead
and create a report here,

00:06:53.847 --> 00:06:55.680
and this will take us
back into Data Studio.

00:06:55.680 --> 00:06:57.405
So this is what Minhaz
was showing previously.

00:06:57.405 --> 00:06:58.863
He was showing
pre-made dashboards.

00:06:58.863 --> 00:07:02.130
This is kind of the editor
view that you would see for it.

00:07:02.130 --> 00:07:03.840
So you'll see here
that all I had to do

00:07:03.840 --> 00:07:05.772
was put in a couple of
configuration items.

00:07:05.772 --> 00:07:07.230
And I already have
a live dashboard

00:07:07.230 --> 00:07:11.332
that's looking for stack
overflow questions.

00:07:11.332 --> 00:07:12.260
And there we go.

00:07:17.232 --> 00:07:18.940
So you might be
wondering what's actually

00:07:18.940 --> 00:07:20.650
going on behind
the scenes when I

00:07:20.650 --> 00:07:22.000
went through all those actions.

00:07:22.000 --> 00:07:24.520
So we have three main
categories that happens

00:07:24.520 --> 00:07:25.810
with this connector flow.

00:07:25.810 --> 00:07:27.226
We have requests
that are actually

00:07:27.226 --> 00:07:28.960
made to your app
script's connector code.

00:07:28.960 --> 00:07:30.649
We have the Data
Studio interface.

00:07:30.649 --> 00:07:32.440
And we have the user
interaction that would

00:07:32.440 --> 00:07:34.384
happen on the interface itself.

00:07:34.384 --> 00:07:35.800
So the first thing
that happens is

00:07:35.800 --> 00:07:38.260
the user selects the
connector in Data Studio.

00:07:38.260 --> 00:07:40.780
That, in my case, was when I
was searching for the Stack

00:07:40.780 --> 00:07:42.400
Overflow connector.

00:07:42.400 --> 00:07:44.410
After that's searched
and selected,

00:07:44.410 --> 00:07:46.120
we just do a quick
call to getAuth

00:07:46.120 --> 00:07:48.280
Type, which is an additional
function you provide

00:07:48.280 --> 00:07:50.655
that just says what sort of
authentication your connector

00:07:50.655 --> 00:07:51.550
needs.

00:07:51.550 --> 00:07:53.470
If necessary, we
trigger an authflow.

00:07:53.470 --> 00:07:55.820
In our case, we had already
authorized that connector,

00:07:55.820 --> 00:07:56.830
so that was unnecessary.

00:07:56.830 --> 00:07:59.320
So we kind of already
completed the authflow.

00:07:59.320 --> 00:08:01.695
The next thing that happens
is a call to getConfig.

00:08:01.695 --> 00:08:03.070
And this is what
actually made it

00:08:03.070 --> 00:08:05.170
where we could see which
configuration items were

00:08:05.170 --> 00:08:05.890
needed.

00:08:05.890 --> 00:08:07.348
In the case of
Stack Overflow, that

00:08:07.348 --> 00:08:10.210
was the tag and
the sort options.

00:08:10.210 --> 00:08:11.920
We share that
configuration interface,

00:08:11.920 --> 00:08:14.057
and we let the user
complete the configuration.

00:08:14.057 --> 00:08:15.640
And then at that
point, we make a call

00:08:15.640 --> 00:08:18.100
to their getSchema
function, and that

00:08:18.100 --> 00:08:22.000
was what was able to show us the
config fields on the next page.

00:08:22.000 --> 00:08:24.250
We show that field
screen, and the user

00:08:24.250 --> 00:08:25.600
can edit those if necessary.

00:08:25.600 --> 00:08:27.016
In our case, we
were already happy

00:08:27.016 --> 00:08:28.720
with the fields that were there.

00:08:28.720 --> 00:08:30.650
And then we're just
into Data Studio.

00:08:30.650 --> 00:08:33.118
At that point, the user can
add any charts as necessary.

00:08:33.118 --> 00:08:34.659
Or in my case, we
had a template that

00:08:34.659 --> 00:08:37.904
already had the charts created,
so it just did a getData call.

00:08:37.904 --> 00:08:39.820
And it brought in all
the data that we needed.

00:08:39.820 --> 00:08:42.320
At this point, Data Studio just
shows a chart with the data.

00:08:44.780 --> 00:08:46.960
So just a quick
thing to mention-- we

00:08:46.960 --> 00:08:50.320
have a Community Connector
Codelab that's live right now.

00:08:50.320 --> 00:08:51.790
You can see it at
this link below.

00:08:51.790 --> 00:08:54.936
And I believe we will also
be sharing that in the chat.

00:08:54.936 --> 00:08:56.560
This Codelab is just
a great way to get

00:08:56.560 --> 00:08:58.042
started with
Community Connectors,

00:08:58.042 --> 00:09:00.250
so that way you can see,
what would you actually need

00:09:00.250 --> 00:09:02.100
to implement it for
your own data source?

00:09:04.789 --> 00:09:07.330
One thing worth mentioning is
how you can share the Community

00:09:07.330 --> 00:09:08.340
Connector.

00:09:08.340 --> 00:09:10.920
So I mentioned earlier
that this is similar to how

00:09:10.920 --> 00:09:12.330
Drive permissions work.

00:09:12.330 --> 00:09:15.450
You can share with just
individual selected people.

00:09:15.450 --> 00:09:17.610
You can share with your
organization, organization

00:09:17.610 --> 00:09:19.740
being a G Suite account,
and you can also share it

00:09:19.740 --> 00:09:20.976
with anyone with a link.

00:09:20.976 --> 00:09:22.350
Anyone with a link
is a great way

00:09:22.350 --> 00:09:24.060
if you want anyone
to be able to use it.

00:09:24.060 --> 00:09:26.435
All you have to do is give
them a link to your connector,

00:09:26.435 --> 00:09:29.350
and they'll be able
to connect to it.

00:09:29.350 --> 00:09:32.340
We also have a new feature that
we launched this week called

00:09:32.340 --> 00:09:33.370
Connector Direct Links.

00:09:33.370 --> 00:09:35.370
And direct links are
something that we're really

00:09:35.370 --> 00:09:36.360
excited about.

00:09:36.360 --> 00:09:38.610
It allows you, as a
connector developer,

00:09:38.610 --> 00:09:40.764
to give someone a link
that's personalized to them

00:09:40.764 --> 00:09:42.930
with the known values that
you can know that they'll

00:09:42.930 --> 00:09:46.047
want ahead of time that also
can customize any templates.

00:09:46.047 --> 00:09:48.130
So I'll show that off right
now through this link.

00:09:55.520 --> 00:09:57.760
So before when we went into
Stack Overflow Questions,

00:09:57.760 --> 00:10:01.122
I had to type in all of
the fields that we needed.

00:10:01.122 --> 00:10:02.580
And actually, we've
had this happen

00:10:02.580 --> 00:10:03.780
a couple of times before.

00:10:03.780 --> 00:10:05.410
Since this is still
a new feature,

00:10:05.410 --> 00:10:06.630
currently it's behind a flag.

00:10:06.630 --> 00:10:08.390
We're going to launch it in
production later this week,

00:10:08.390 --> 00:10:09.520
so it's not working.

00:10:09.520 --> 00:10:10.740
But if this would have
been working correctly,

00:10:10.740 --> 00:10:12.281
the Google Data
Studio tag would have

00:10:12.281 --> 00:10:14.970
been filled in, along with
the result count and the sort.

00:10:14.970 --> 00:10:16.620
MINHAZ KAZI: So anyone
could just click the link,

00:10:16.620 --> 00:10:18.120
and they don't have to
put in the configuration.

00:10:18.120 --> 00:10:18.390
MATT HAMRICK: Yes.

00:10:18.390 --> 00:10:19.380
MINHAZ KAZI: It will be
already configured for them.

00:10:19.380 --> 00:10:21.840
MATT HAMRICK: So normally
in this case, this demo,

00:10:21.840 --> 00:10:23.820
these fields up here
would have been filled in.

00:10:23.820 --> 00:10:26.195
But since it isn't actually
live quite in production yet,

00:10:26.195 --> 00:10:28.270
there's small
technical flub there.

00:10:33.210 --> 00:10:35.856
So one of the things that's nice
about the way we have this set

00:10:35.856 --> 00:10:37.730
up is there is also a
publishing model that's

00:10:37.730 --> 00:10:41.040
friendly to individuals
or companies.

00:10:41.040 --> 00:10:43.507
For individuals, one of the
easiest things that they can do

00:10:43.507 --> 00:10:45.840
is they can just publish as
is an open source connector.

00:10:45.840 --> 00:10:49.790
This allows them to have the
maintenance cost to be put off,

00:10:49.790 --> 00:10:52.280
where we'll handle making sure
that that's taken care of.

00:10:52.280 --> 00:10:55.025
There's also the ability to
publish as a partner maintains.

00:10:55.025 --> 00:10:57.860
So this is saying you'll
maintain your own connector,

00:10:57.860 --> 00:10:59.630
but you'll also have
all of the freedom

00:10:59.630 --> 00:11:01.460
to do any sort of
monetization schemes

00:11:01.460 --> 00:11:03.620
that make sense
for your use case.

00:11:03.620 --> 00:11:06.090
All of these guidelines are
available on our developer

00:11:06.090 --> 00:11:06.750
site.

00:11:06.750 --> 00:11:08.420
And I believe that link will
also be shared in the chat

00:11:08.420 --> 00:11:09.320
below.

00:11:09.320 --> 00:11:11.417
Regardless of choice,
this makes a simple way

00:11:11.417 --> 00:11:13.250
where you'll be able
to share your Community

00:11:13.250 --> 00:11:16.660
Connector with anyone
that needs to see it.

00:11:16.660 --> 00:11:18.520
Next slide, we'll be
going back to Minhaz

00:11:18.520 --> 00:11:20.570
for how Data Studio can be
used to analyze your data.

00:11:20.570 --> 00:11:22.270
