00:29:12.950 --> 00:29:14.320
So we have a page for that.

00:29:14.320 --> 00:29:18.990
And if we go in quickly--

00:29:23.764 --> 00:29:24.870
oh, all right.

00:29:24.870 --> 00:29:27.476
So we'll bring that up later.

00:29:27.476 --> 00:29:31.220
The second question is, what
are the ways Data Studio creates

00:29:31.220 --> 00:29:34.070
simple reports from
databases such as MySQL

00:29:34.070 --> 00:29:36.080
via store procedures?

00:29:36.080 --> 00:29:38.550
So if you already have a
store procedure in MySQL

00:29:38.550 --> 00:29:40.820
and if you have a
view based on that,

00:29:40.820 --> 00:29:44.720
you can simply connect to
MySQL using the MySQL connector

00:29:44.720 --> 00:29:47.390
and then connect to that view.

00:29:47.390 --> 00:29:51.970
Currently, we don't support
passing in parameters from Data

00:29:51.970 --> 00:29:53.565
Studio to that store procedure.

00:29:53.565 --> 00:29:55.190
But that's something
we're looking into

00:29:55.190 --> 00:29:57.170
and might be supported
in the future.

00:29:57.170 --> 00:30:00.560
But if you have a view that
uses the MySQL store procedure

00:30:00.560 --> 00:30:02.660
and doesn't require
a parameter input,

00:30:02.660 --> 00:30:04.950
then you can just use that view.

00:30:04.950 --> 00:30:07.850
Third question is, how does
one stop Data Studio reports

00:30:07.850 --> 00:30:10.620
from running queries
in the background?

00:30:10.620 --> 00:30:14.709
So Data Studio doesn't really
run queries in the background.

00:30:14.709 --> 00:30:16.750
There are several times
when it will run queries.

00:30:16.750 --> 00:30:20.980
First is that if it knows
that there are certain queries

00:30:20.980 --> 00:30:24.950
it can make beforehand that
will speed up your report,

00:30:24.950 --> 00:30:27.240
then even before
you open the report,

00:30:27.240 --> 00:30:29.784
it'll sort of pre-fetch
some queries and store them.

00:30:29.784 --> 00:30:32.200
The next time you open the
report, it won't run the query.

00:30:32.200 --> 00:30:33.980
It will just fetch
from the cache.

00:30:33.980 --> 00:30:36.740
When you're building a
report, every time you

00:30:36.740 --> 00:30:41.690
add a component or every
time you edit a database

00:30:41.690 --> 00:30:44.220
that requests Data
Studio to get new data,

00:30:44.220 --> 00:30:49.080
then it will create a new query
and then pull that data in.

00:30:49.080 --> 00:30:52.020
If you're viewing a dashboard
and the results are already

00:30:52.020 --> 00:30:55.320
in the cache, then it
won't run the query.

00:30:55.320 --> 00:30:57.870
It will just fetch the
data from the cache.

00:30:57.870 --> 00:30:59.370
If you're the owner
of the dashboard

00:30:59.370 --> 00:31:01.540
and if you manually
refresh the data,

00:31:01.540 --> 00:31:04.450
then it will run
the query again.

00:31:04.450 --> 00:31:07.310
If you're having issues with
multiple queries running--

00:31:07.310 --> 00:31:10.010
let's say if you're connecting
to a BigQuery dataset,

00:31:10.010 --> 00:31:12.940
and if you're getting a lot of
queries, one thing you could do

00:31:12.940 --> 00:31:15.370
is maybe you can create
a Community Connector

00:31:15.370 --> 00:31:18.160
and set up your
own caching ware.

00:31:18.160 --> 00:31:19.480
You can cache to Firebase.

00:31:19.480 --> 00:31:22.510
You can cache to Cloud SQL.

00:31:22.510 --> 00:31:25.840
And then you can set up logic
where the first time the data

00:31:25.840 --> 00:31:27.887
is being pulled, it
will pull from BigQuery

00:31:27.887 --> 00:31:29.470
and then cache it
in the middle layer.

00:31:29.470 --> 00:31:32.660
And then the following
times the query is run,

00:31:32.660 --> 00:31:34.480
it will fetch from that cache.

00:31:34.480 --> 00:31:38.075
And that will significantly
save on your query costs.

00:31:38.075 --> 00:31:40.130
This fourth question
we have is, can one

00:31:40.130 --> 00:31:42.474
connect Google Cloud Datastore
to Google Data Studio?

00:31:42.474 --> 00:31:44.390
MATT HAMRICK: I'll go
ahead and take this one.

00:31:44.390 --> 00:31:45.200
MINHAZ KAZI: Sure.

00:31:45.200 --> 00:31:47.280
MATT HAMRICK: So the
short answer is yes,

00:31:47.280 --> 00:31:50.160
and the slightly longer
answer is not right now.

00:31:50.160 --> 00:31:51.759
So the reason the
answer is yes is

00:31:51.759 --> 00:31:54.050
because we can make a Community
Connector that connects

00:31:54.050 --> 00:31:55.929
to Google Cloud Datastore.

00:31:55.929 --> 00:31:58.220
I don't think we currently
have a first-party connector

00:31:58.220 --> 00:32:00.320
to Google Cloud
Datastore, but that

00:32:00.320 --> 00:32:02.330
would be a great one to put.

00:32:02.330 --> 00:32:03.997
So if somebody is
struck by inspiration,

00:32:03.997 --> 00:32:05.371
that would be a
good one to make.

00:32:05.371 --> 00:32:07.300
And then go ahead and
give us a pull request

00:32:07.300 --> 00:32:08.850
on our GitHub repo.

00:32:08.850 --> 00:32:10.100
MINHAZ KAZI: That sounds good.

00:32:10.100 --> 00:32:13.790
And the last
question we have is,

00:32:13.790 --> 00:32:16.040
can one export report to Sheets?

00:32:16.040 --> 00:32:17.840
You can't export
the whole report.

00:32:17.840 --> 00:32:20.720
Like if you have, let's
say, a 10-page dashboard

00:32:20.720 --> 00:32:23.540
where each page has
five or six elements,

00:32:23.540 --> 00:32:26.020
you can't export the
whole thing as a whole.

00:32:26.020 --> 00:32:28.410
But what you can do
is on a dashboard,

00:32:28.410 --> 00:32:31.400
if you have different elements,
if you hover over an element,

00:32:31.400 --> 00:32:34.765
there'll be a small icon that
will come up on the top right.

00:32:34.765 --> 00:32:36.140
And if you click
on it, you'll be

00:32:36.140 --> 00:32:39.890
able to export the underlying
data for that element

00:32:39.890 --> 00:32:40.890
to the Sheet.

00:32:40.890 --> 00:32:42.830
So if you click Export
to Sheet, whatever

00:32:42.830 --> 00:32:46.400
data was used to
create that report

00:32:46.400 --> 00:32:50.540
or create this one element
will get exported into a Sheet

00:32:50.540 --> 00:32:53.840
where you can use that data
and then create your own chart

00:32:53.840 --> 00:32:59.380
or graph in Sheet, or maybe
even add additional data to it.

00:32:59.380 --> 00:33:03.350
And that's all the
Q&amp;A we have for now.

